course: biology
lesson: 207
title: Horses and ponies: how they eat, see, sleep and live in herds
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A horse is a big, strong animal, but in the wild it was always someone's dinner, so almost everything about it is built for spotting danger and running away.
explain: Its eyes sit on the sides of its head so it can see nearly all the way round, and when one horse in a field suddenly bolts, the whole herd thunders after it without stopping to ask why.
explain: A horse can even doze on its feet: special locking joints in its legs hold it up without effort, so it is ready to run the moment it wakes, although it still lies down for short spells of deep sleep.
explain: Horses also have small stomachs and graze for most of the day, and they talk to each other mostly with their ears, eyes and bodies rather than with sounds.
fact: Horses can doze standing up because locking joints in their legs hold them without effort, but they must lie down for short periods to get deep sleep.
fact: A horse's stomach is small, so it eats grass and hay for many hours a day, about two kilograms of food for every hundred kilograms it weighs.
fact: Sugar lumps are bad for horses: too much sugar harms teeth and can bring on a painful foot illness, and a slice of apple or carrot held on a flat hand, so fingers do not get nipped by mistake, is a better treat.
fact: A horse's eyes on the sides of its head let it see almost all round, but it has blind spots right in front of its nose and right behind it, so a horse surprised from behind may kick out in fright.
fact: Ears pricked forward mean a horse is interested; ears pinned flat back are a warning that it is angry or annoyed.
fact: Ponies are horses below a certain height, often sturdy, hardy and clever, and Shetland ponies from cold Scottish islands grow thick winter coats.
fact: Horses are herd animals: running away together kept wild horses safe, and a horse kept alone gets stressed, so it needs a companion.
fact: Horses roll to scratch itches and care for their coats, often after a sweaty ride, and a coat of mud helps keep flies off.
fact: A foal stands and walks within a few hours of birth, on legs that are very long for its body, because a wild foal must keep up with the herd.
fact: When a horse curls up its top lip and shows its teeth, it is not laughing: this is called flehmen, and it pushes a smell up to a special sense organ in the roof of the mouth, as cats and goats also do.
fact: Horse teeth keep growing out of the jaw and are worn down by grinding grass, so their shape tells roughly how old a horse is; that is why people say «don't look a gift horse in the mouth».
exercise: How can a horse doze standing up? | Locking joints in its legs hold it up without effort | It leans its whole body against the fence all night long | It never really sleeps at all | It sleeps standing only in winter
exercise: Does a horse ever need to lie down to sleep? | Yes, for short spells of deep sleep | No, never in its whole life | Only when it is a foal | Only when it is ill
exercise: Why does a horse graze for most of the day? | Its stomach is small and needs food passing through nearly all the time | It is always starving because grass has no food in it | It has to eat its own weight every day | It is trying to keep the grass short for the farmer
exercise: Which treat is better for a horse than a sugar lump? | A slice of apple or carrot on a flat hand | A bar of chocolate | A handful of crisps | A big spoonful of honey from a jar, straight off the spoon
exercise: Why should you hold your hand flat when you feed a horse? | So it does not nip your fingers by mistake | So the horse can see the lines on your palm and know you | So the treat stays warm | So the horse knows it is the last one
exercise: Where are a horse's blind spots? | Right in front of its nose and right behind it | Only on its left side | Straight above its head and nowhere else at all | It has no blind spots at all, because its eyes see everywhere
exercise: Why are a horse's eyes on the sides of its head? | To spot danger coming from almost any direction | To see colours better than us | To read signs on both sides of a road at the same time | To watch two foals at once
exercise: What do a horse's ears pinned flat back mean? | It is angry or annoyed | It is happy and relaxed | It is listening to music | It is about to fall asleep
exercise: What is a pony? | A horse below a certain height | A baby horse that is not yet grown | A cross between a horse and a donkey | A horse that never grows a mane
exercise: Why do Shetland ponies have thick coats? | They come from cold Scottish islands | They are always about to have foals | Their coats are stuffed for shows | They come from hot deserts
exercise: Why does a whole herd run when one horse gets scared? | Running together kept wild horses safe | Horses love racing each other | The scared horse is always the boss of the herd, so the rest obey it | The others want its food
exercise: Why should a horse not be kept completely alone? | It is a herd animal and gets stressed without company | It will forget how to walk | It will start to bark like a dog to call other animals to it | It will stop growing its mane and tail
exercise: Why do horses roll in mud? | To scratch itches and keep flies off | To cool their hooves only | To hide from the farmer | To make their coat shiny for shows
exercise: Why can a foal walk within hours of birth? | A wild foal must keep up with the herd | Its mother carries it on her back until then | Foals are born already a whole year old | The farmer teaches it in the first hour
exercise: What is a horse doing when it curls up its top lip? | Pushing a smell to a special sense organ in its mouth | Laughing at a joke | Showing it is about to bite whoever is standing in front of it | Cleaning its front teeth
exercise: How can people roughly tell a horse's age? | From the shape and wear of its teeth | From the length and thickness of its tail | From the colour of the hooves on its feet | From how loudly it neighs when it is hungry
test: «my cousin says horses never sleep cos they're always standing lol» What is right? | They doze standing up but lie down for short deep sleeps | They never sleep at all | They only sleep in water | They sleep standing for a whole week, then lie down for one long night
test: «gave the pony my whole bag of sugar lumps, he loved it» What is the problem? | Too much sugar harms horses' teeth and feet | Sugar makes ponies grow too tall | Nothing, sugar is a horse's best food | Sugar makes horses sleep standing up
test: «just gonna run up behind the horse and surprise it» What would most likely happen? | It cannot see you there and may kick out in fright | It will turn and lick you | Nothing, horses see straight behind them perfectly well | It will lie down to let you ride
test: In a cartoon a horse laughs and shows all its teeth at a joke. What is a real horse doing with a curled lip? | Smelling something with a special organ in its mouth | Laughing like a person at a funny joke | Crying because it is sad | Getting ready to sing a song to the other horses in the field
test: «3 horses n a pony in the field, how many legs» | 16 | 12 | 8 | 20
test: «horse, donkey and 2 foals, how many ears, no wait 3 foals» | 10 | 8 | 4 | 12
test: What would happen if a lone horse was kept in a field with no other animal for months? | It would likely get stressed and unhappy | It would be happier than in a herd | It would start eating meat | It would forget it is a horse
test: «bloody horse just bolted cos a bird flew up, and the rest followed, stupid lot» Why did they all run? | Horses are prey animals, and running together kept them safe | Horses hate birds and chase them away from the field whenever they can | The first horse was the farmer's | They were racing for the gate
test: A newborn foal on a farm is standing up on wobbly long legs after an hour. Is that normal? | Yes, foals stand and walk within a few hours | No, it should lie still in the straw for a month | No, foals cannot stand until they are a year old | Yes, but only tiny Shetland foals can do that
test: A horse turns its ears forward towards you and watches. What does this tell you? | It is interested in you | It is about to kick you hard | It is angry and warning you | It cannot hear anything at all
test: In My Little Pony the ponies have wings, horns and bright pink manes. What is true of real ponies? | They are short, sturdy horses with ordinary coats and manes | They are young horses that later grow wings and horns when they are older | They live only on rainbows | They are a kind of unicorn
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the sleeping habits of the domestic horse? | The animal may doze erect by means of locking joints, yet requires brief recumbent periods for deep sleep | The horse abstains from sleep entirely | The horse sleeps exclusively while lying down for twelve hours | The horse sleeps only during the winter months
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the horse grazes for the greater part of the day. | The stomach is of small capacity and requires a near-continuous passage of food | Grass contains no nourishment whatsoever, so vast quantities must be consumed to survive | The animal is required to keep pasture short | Grazing serves solely to sharpen the teeth
exam: It is given that a horse has its ears laid flat against its head. Ascertain the correct interpretation. | The animal is angered or annoyed and is issuing a warning | The animal is content and relaxed | The animal is attending to distant music | The animal is about to fall asleep
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the positioning of the equine eye. | Lateral eyes afford near all-round vision, with blind regions before the nose and behind the body | The eyes afford no vision to the side | The horse perceives everything clearly directly behind itself and above its head | The positioning of the eyes serves solely to discern colour
